Let $\left(x_{1}, y_{1}\right)$ be one of the points of contact. Given curve is $y=\cos x$

$$

\begin{array}{l}

\Rightarrow \frac{\mathrm{dy}}{\mathrm{dx}}=-\sin \mathrm{x} \\

\left.\Rightarrow \frac{\mathrm{dy}}{\mathrm{dx}}\right|_{\left(\mathrm{x}_{1}, \mathrm{y}_{1}\right)}=-\sin \mathrm{x}_{1}

\end{array}

$$

Now the equation of the tangent at $\left(\mathrm{x}_{1}, \mathrm{y}_{1}\right)$ is

$$

\begin{aligned}

& y-y_{1}\left(\frac{d y}{d x}\right)_{\left(x_{1}, y_{1}\right)}\left(x-x_{1}\right) \\

\Rightarrow & y-y_{1}=-\sin x_{1}\left(0-x_{1}\right)

\end{aligned}

$$

Since, it is given that equation of tangent passes through origin.

$$

\begin{array}{lr}

\therefore & 0-y_{1}=-\sin x_{1}\left(0-x_{1}\right) \\

\Rightarrow & y_{1}=-x_{1} \sin x_{1}

\end{array}

$$

Also, point $\left(x_{1}, y_{1}\right)$ lies on $y=\cos x$

$$

\therefore \quad y_{1}=\cos x_{1}

$$

From Eqs. (i), (ii), we get

$$

\begin{aligned}

& \sin ^{2} x_{1}+\cos ^{2} x_{1}=\frac{y_{1}^{2}}{x_{1}^{2}}+y_{1}^{2}=1 \\

\Rightarrow & x_{1}^{2}=y_{1}^{2}+y_{1}^{2} x_{1}^{2}

\end{aligned}

$$

Hence, the locus of $\left(x_{1}, y_{1}\right)$ is

$$

x^{2}=y^{2}+y^{2} x^{2} \Rightarrow x^{2} y^{2}=x^{2}-y^{2}

$$
